.TH XYMONPING 1 "Version 4.3.30:  4 Sep 2019" "Xymon"
.SH NAME
xymonping \- Xymon ping tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "xymonping [\-\-retries=N] [\-\-timeout=N] [\-\-samples=N [\-\-per\-host\-interval=MS]] [IP\-addresses]"

.SH DESCRIPTION
.I xymonping(1)
is used for ping testing of the hosts monitored by the
.I xymon(7)
monitoring system. It reads a list of IP addresses from stdin,
and performs a "ping" check to see if these hosts are alive.
It is normally invoked by the
.I xymonnet(1)
utility, which performs all of the Xymon network tests.

Optionally, if a list of IP-addresses is passed as command-line
arguments, it will ping those IP's instead of reading them
from stdin.

xymonping only handles IP-addresses, not hostnames: IPv4, or IPv6 with
a link-local address given as addr%interface. At most 65535
addresses are pinged in one run; the rest are refused with an error.

xymonping was inspired by the
.I fping(1)
tool, but has been written from scratch to implement a fast
ping tester without much of the overhead found in other
such utilities. The output from xymonping is similar to that
of "fping \-Ae".

xymonping probes multiple systems in parallel, sending at most \-\-max\-pps
packets per second, so a run takes about the number of hosts divided by
that rate, plus the time the slowest host takes to answer or to be given
up on (about 4 seconds with the default options).

.SH PRIVILEGES
xymonping needs to send and receive ICMP Echo (ping) packets. It first
tries an unprivileged ICMP socket (SOCK_DGRAM). Linux allows one to a
user whose group is inside the
.B net.ipv4.ping_group_range
sysctl; many distributions set that range to cover every group. To
allow it everywhere:
.sp
.nf
\&   sysctl \-w net.ipv4.ping_group_range="0 2147483647"
.fi
.PP
If that socket is refused, xymonping falls back to a raw socket, which
needs root: install xymonping suid-root. It drops root privileges
immediately, and only regains them to open the raw socket; no
user-supplied or network data is handled while privileged.
.PP
File capabilities work as well: \fBsetcap cap_net_raw+ep\fR on the
binary lets xymonping open the raw socket without being suid-root.
.PP
IPv6 addresses need the unprivileged ICMPv6 socket or root in the same
way.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP "\fB\-\-retries\fR=\fIN\fR"
Sets the number of retries for hosts that fail to respond to
the initial ping, i.e. the number of ping probes sent in addition
to the initial probe. The default is \-\-retries=3, as with fping, to ping a host
4 times before concluding that it is not responding.

.IP "\fB\-\-timeout\fR=\fIN\fR"
Determines the timeout (in seconds) for the first ping to a host; each
retry waits 1.5 times longer than the one before, as fping's \-t and \-B
do. A reply that arrives later than its own ping's timeout does not count.
Without this option the first ping waits 500 ms, so a host that never
answers is given up on after about 4 seconds (500, 750, 1125 and
1688 ms), and one whose replies take longer than that is reported
unreachable: \-\-timeout=5 gives such a link back the old 5 seconds a try.
With \-\-samples every ping gets the same timeout: by default the
\-\-per\-host\-interval, at most 2 seconds, as in fping's count mode.

.IP "\fB\-\-responses\fR=\fIN\fR"
xymonping normally stops pinging a host after receiving a single
response, and uses that to determine the round-trip time. If the
first response takes longer to arrive - e.g. because of additional
network overhead when first determining the route to the target host -
it may skew the round-trip-time reports. You can then use this option
to require N responses, and xymonping will calculate the round-trip
time as the average of all of responsetimes.

.IP "\fB\-\-samples\fR=\fIN\fR"
Enables SmokePing-style probing: sends exactly N pings to each host (N at
most 1000), without retries, and records every round-trip time rather
than only the average. The pings to one host are spaced by
\-\-per\-host\-interval, so
each host gets one train of N pings per run. The output line for each
host gains fields after the usual text, so that existing parsers keep
working: an alive host gets the samples and the loss, an unreachable one
the loss alone:
.sp
.nf
\&  1.2.3.4 is alive (311 usec) samples=0.000310,0.000320,0.000305 loss=0/3
\&  1.2.3.5 is unreachable loss=3/3
.fi
.IP
Samples are in seconds; loss is reported as lost/total. A ping counts as
lost when no reply arrives within \-\-timeout of sending it. Without this
option (or with N=0) the output is unchanged.
xymonnet(1) runs xymonping this way for hosts carrying the "smoke" tag
in hosts.cfg(5).
.IP "\fB\-\-per\-host\-interval\fR=\fIMS\fR"
With \-\-samples, the time in milliseconds between two pings to the
same host. The default is 1000, as with fping's \-p option and
SmokePing: the N pings form a train of (N\-1) seconds. Pings to
different hosts are interleaved within that spacing, and no two pings
leave closer together than \-\-max\-pps allows.
.IP "\fB\-\-max\-pps\fR=\fIN\fR"
Maximum number of packets per second. This limits the number of
ICMP packets xymonping will send per second, by enforcing a
brief delay after each packet is sent. The default setting is
to send a maximum of 100 packets per second, fping's default. Note that increasing
this may cause flooding of the network, and since ICMP packets
can be discarded by routers and other network equipment, this
can cause erratic behaviour with hosts recorded as not responding
when they are in fact OK.

.IP "\fB\-\-source\fR=\fIADDRESS\fR"
Use ADDRESS as the source IP address of the ping packets sent.
On multi-homed systems, allows you to select the source IP of
the hosts going out, which might be necessary for ping to work.

.IP "\fB\-i\fR\fIMS\fR, \fB\-r\fR\fIN\fR, \fB\-t\fR\fIMS\fR, \fB\-S\fR\fIADDRESS\fR"
The fping options xymonping accepts, meaning what they mean to fping: the
least time in milliseconds between two pings, the retries, the first
ping's timeout in milliseconds, and the source address. Any other option
starting with \- is ignored.

.IP "\fB\-\-debug\fR"
Enable debug output. This prints out all packets sent and received.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymon(7), xymonnet(1), fping(1)

